  "prediction": "The report outlines the Atomic Energy Act (AEA) of 1954, as amended, and its provisions regarding nuclear cooperation agreements between the United States and other countries. The AEA mandates a legislative process for approving such agreements, which includes submission to Congress for a period of 90 days.\n\nThe report highlights several instances where nuclear cooperation agreements were approved outside of the AEA process. For example, in 1985, President Reagan submitted the first U.S.-China nuclear cooperation agreement to Congress, which was approved through a joint resolution. In 2006, the U.S. Congress passed P.L. 109-401, which permitted the President to waive certain provisions of the AEA for a nuclear cooperation agreement with India.\n\nThe report also mentions that there have been instances where nuclear cooperation agreements were approved without sufficient legislative days remaining in a Congressional session. In such cases, Congress has passed legislation to approve the agreements retroactively.\n\nThe report concludes by noting that the AEA sets out the legislative process for approving nuclear cooperation agreements between the United States and other countries, but there have been instances where these agreements have been approved outside of this process.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report outlines the Atomic Energy Act (AEA) of 1954, as amended, and its provisions regarding nuclear cooperation agreements between the United States and other countries. The AEA mandates a legislative process for approving such agreements, which includes submission to Congress for a period of 90 days.\n\nThe report highlights several instances where nuclear cooperation agreements were approved outside of the AEA process. For example, in 1985, President Reagan submitted the first U.S.-China nuclear cooperation agreement to Congress, which was approved through a joint resolution. In 2006, the U.S. Congress passed P.L. 109-401, which permitted the President to waive certain provisions of the AEA for a nuclear cooperation agreement with India.\n\nThe report also mentions that there have been instances where nuclear cooperation agreements were approved without sufficient legislative days remaining in a Congressional session. In such cases, Congress has passed legislation to approve the agreements retroactively.\n\nThe report concludes by noting that the AEA sets out the legislative process for approving nuclear cooperation agreements between the United States and other countries, but there have been instances where these agreements have been approved outside of this process.",
  "score": 28.571428091907602,
  "answers": [
    "In order for the United States to engage in significant civilian nuclear cooperation with other states, it must conclude a framework agreement that meets specific requirements under Section 123 of the Atomic Energy Act (AEA). Significant nuclear cooperation includes the export of reactors, critical parts of reactors, and reactor fuel. The AEA also provides for export control licensing procedures and criteria for terminating cooperation. Congressional review is required for Section 123 agreements; the AEA establishes special parliamentary procedures by which Congress may act on a proposed agreement."
  ],
